<section class="page-head">
  <h1>A fail-closed AI pipeline for regulated customer content</h1>
  <p class="section-note">Turning third-party-assured lifecycle data into localized, design-ready product carbon-footprint fact sheets, where every number has to survive legal review.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study">
  <p class="masked-note">
    This case study has been deliberately masked. Product and program names, specific claims,
    and internal tooling details have been removed to protect confidential information.
    What remains is the shape of the problem, the approach, and the headline results.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Across a broad device portfolio, we publish product carbon-footprint (PCF) fact sheets —
    customer-facing documents that summarize a product's environmental impact. Producing them
    is design-heavy, and scaling them beyond a single market multiplies the effort: every
    product, every locale, is a fresh round of manual layout and copy. The design work — not
    the data — was the bottleneck to scaling.
  </p>

  <h2>How I framed it</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the work was repeatable: the same assured lifecycle data flowing into the same
    document structure, over and over. That's a job for deterministic code, not human hours —
    and not, on its own, for an autonomous agent. The genuine judgment lived at the edges —
    interpreting the inputs, the imagery, and the final compliance call. So I designed a pipeline
    with a <strong>deterministic core</strong> and kept those judgment calls with people.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <ul class="case-steps">
    <li><strong>Ingest</strong> third-party-assured LCA results into the tool as the single source of truth.</li>
    <li><strong>Match</strong> that data to pre-cleared, approved claim language — deterministically, so the substance is never invented.</li>
    <li><strong>Assemble deterministically</strong> — populate the approved content into the layout by ID, not by model discretion.</li>
    <li><strong>Push to Figma via MCP</strong> to generate the design elements programmatically, instead of rebuilding each sheet by hand in external design software.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One rule held throughout: automation could shape the <em>form</em> — layout, assembly,
    formatting — but never the <em>substance</em>. Every published number and claim traces back
    to the assured data and pre-approved language, validation stayed deterministic, and the
    judgment calls — the data, the imagery, the final compliance read — stayed with people. In a
    compliance-sensitive domain, that boundary isn't optional.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <img src="/img/petiscos-pipeline.svg" alt="Pipeline diagram. The automated path is deterministic: ingest assured LCA data and approved claims, fill and localize the numbers, pass a fail-closed QA gate, then render into Figma by ID and export PDFs. The QA gate re-runs on failure and, after three attempts, escalates to a human and blocks publish. Humans own the judgment calls: providing the data, placing per-locale images, and final review and publish." />
    <figcaption>The shape of it: a deterministic pipeline with humans on the judgment calls. Code fills approved numbers and wording, and a fail-closed gate keeps everything tied back to assured data — escalating to a human after three tries, never shipping a failure. People own the data, the imagery, and the final publish. No autonomous agent, on purpose.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What it changed</h2>
  <p>
    In 2026, 28 of 28 fact sheets across 14 programs cleared legal, PR, and GTM review with zero
    corrections. Production time per program fell 51%, hitting the 50% goal, and the production
    budget fell by over 90%: the fall launch needed no temporary staff, and rendering into Figma
    via MCP eliminated the need for an external design-platform contract. The pipeline is built for
    14 marketplaces and 32 locales; 2026 was deliberately scoped to US English and Spanish to
    prove quality first.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest caveat</h2>
  <p>
    It's not magic, and it's not done. Human review still owns the final call — especially
    anything a regulator could read. The value wasn't replacing judgment; it was clearing away
    the repeatable design labor so people could spend their time on the parts that genuinely
    need a human.
  </p>

